WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190
- Adam is the most excited about this episode.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.530
In fact, it was his idea, so toast!

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.450
- That's right.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.060
We were pretty excited to try

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.113
a bunch of bread for an episode.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.280
Adam had the brilliant idea

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.620
of looking at bread world through the ship of toast.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.680
So today, not only are we going to three restaurants,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.260
but we're also going to a couple of bakeries.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.610
This is really going to be a jam-packed episode.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:23.443
- Wow.

00:00:23.443 --> 00:00:24.600
Today on Worth It,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.040
we're gonna be trying three awesome toast dishes

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.080
at three drastically,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.880
wait, not so drastically different price points,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.480
to find which one is the most worth it at its price.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.620
FYI, the toast we're doing, it's not your average toast.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.660
It's not your side toast, this toast is entree toast.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:39.493
You ready?

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.523
- Toast me.

00:00:41.319 --> 00:00:44.122
(whimsical music)

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.394
^- I'm Jessica Koslow.

00:00:45.394 --> 00:00:47.200
^We're here at Sqirl in Silver Lakes.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.160
Today we're trying our ricotta toast,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.703
the poster child of sweet food here at Sqirl.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.200
Sqirl started in 2011 as a jam company,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.270
and when we started, we really only had this space here.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.460
So we were doing pretty much everything on toast.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.683
All the jams are made here, fresh in copper pans.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.100
It is all labor, the jams are made by hand,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.480
the ricotta, and all of these things.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.110
The only thing that's not is the bread

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.470
because we don't have the space,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.031
and that is why we find really special producers

00:01:12.031 --> 00:01:15.510
like Oven Grandmas for our whitefish tartine.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.340
We use Clark Street Baguettes for

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.870
this chickpea dish that has basically

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.080
a sword where we cut the baguette from tip to tail.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.070
For the ricotta toast, we use a fluffy brioche

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.403
that we get from Farmshop.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.340
- [Anthony] Brioche has actually pretty simple ingredients.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.860
It has flour, yeast, salt, sugar

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.910
^and then the enrichment part, there's milk,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:34.970
there's eggs, and there's butter.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.500
We are using California butter.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.080
It's high butter fat, so you're looking at

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.054
like, 80 percent. We're also using

00:01:39.054 --> 00:01:41.600
California cage-free eggs, and we will shape it,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.130
it will go into the pre-sprayed pans

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.600
that we've got ready to go, our loaf pans,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.680
where you have a dome-top

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.570
which is what the ones we're making for Sqirl.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.320
- And the challenge of making the round-top brioche

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.130
versus the flat pullman loaves

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.010
It's the mixing process. So if the mixing

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.210
isn't done enough, then you're gonna have gaps.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.830
And depending on how the bread proofs, you might have holes.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.560
It's a different game in a flat-base brioche.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.540
- [Voiceover] And then, it is packed early in the morning.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.780
And then it'll get shipped.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:03.900
- [Jessica] Cut size is pretty thick.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.781
We roll it on a butter wheel, so we butter it on both sides,

00:02:05.781 --> 00:02:09.250
and then we put into a panini press.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.980
It's a flat panini press on both sides,

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.910
so it's warm, it's griddled, and then

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.520
we let it rest for a second, we put the ricotta on.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.100
We make it from Straus Organic Cream.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.540
We strain it, we add about 90 percent cream

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.150
by weight back into the ricotta, and we whip it by hand.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.930
And so it is a fluffy type of ricotta.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.830
A lot of times people want a rainbow,

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.560
so they get three flavors of jam.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.920
It does rotate with the seasons, so right now

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.430
we'll do blueberry rhubarb, raspberry cardamom,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.320
strawberry rose geranium. We finish it

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.318
with a squeeze of fresh lemon juice,

00:02:40.318 --> 00:02:43.160
and a sprinkle of Maldon sea salt.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.063
- [Steven] So, at Farmshop,

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.270
they were saying you're the only one

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.693
who doesn't have the flat top bread, why?

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.740
- [Jessica] It looks like a mushroom, it's really fun.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.410
You know, if you're paying eight dollars

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.236
for a ricotta toast, it better look fun

00:02:55.236 --> 00:02:57.663
and it better look ridiculous, and big,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.240
and that's exactly what it does.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.017
- So first of all, cheers, this is a

00:03:01.017 --> 00:03:01.863
- Tumeric Tonic.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.103
- Tumeric Tonic.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.093
That's nice.

00:03:06.093 --> 00:03:08.233
- Is it ginger? Oh, it's so good.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.187
- Tumeric.

00:03:09.187 --> 00:03:10.440
- [Andrew] Look at this toast.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.623
- Whoa, I just got a big whiff of the jam.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.113
- Yeah, this is beyond toast.

00:03:15.113 --> 00:03:18.983
This is my actual face, look at the scale.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.240
Alright, shall I cut you a little cube?

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.073
- Please.

00:03:22.073 --> 00:03:24.810
- [Steven] Whoa. I will say I've never seen

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.390
ricotta presented in this fashion.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.223
- Toast.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:29.943
Nailed it.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:33.103
Oh my god.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.350
- Of all of the elements of this toast,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:38.543
the toast is really just the presenter here.

00:03:38.598 --> 00:03:39.431
- Uh huh.

00:03:39.431 --> 00:03:40.550
- Like, it's amazing, it's doing its job.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.294
The ricotta is insane, it's so

00:03:43.294 --> 00:03:45.950
it's whipped so lightly,

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:46.783
- Yes.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.033
- and it's cheese.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.783
- Okay, next jam.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:55.323
That jam.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:57.450
This is a jam story.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.283
- Yes.

00:03:58.533 --> 00:04:00.820
- This is like, when you're reading Red Riding Hood,

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:01.830
and you think it's about a little girl,

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.530
but really it's about a bad-ass wolf.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.980
- It's like the best parts of a jam sandwich

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.773
that I had, and french toast,

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:10.273
and cake.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.313
But light, like whipped cream.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.860
Mix that in a bottle, and you got ricotta toast!

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.770
Look at the toast, I love the edges of toast.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:20.953
You get the crust.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.623
- The crust is a must. Super delicious.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:24.803
- It's Adam's turn.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:28.810
^- That is so good.

00:04:29.631 --> 00:04:31.793
Ricotta toast.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:32.963
- On brioche.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:34.243
- With jam.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:35.600
- At Sqirl Cafe.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.790
- Is it called Sqirl Cafe?

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:37.880
I don't know, but I'm really into this,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:38.930
like, back-and-forth thing,

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.340
where we just build a sentence together.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.173
I

00:04:41.173 --> 00:04:42.006
- Love

00:04:42.006 --> 00:04:42.839
- Toast!

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.100
- Boom. We're here in

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.400
- Koreatown

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.410
And we're going to a restaurant

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.133
called Here's Looking at You.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.330
Whoa, did you look at me right when I said that?

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.990
- I thought I was gonna have to finish part of the

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.220
- Oh, my bad. Here's looking at--

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.053
- You.

00:04:56.053 --> 00:04:59.810
- There we go, gonna have some pâté, some cheese

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.023
- A savory toast, that's better than most.

00:05:03.023 --> 00:05:05.254
(smooth jazz music)

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.103
- [Jonathan] I'm Jonathan Whitener,

00:05:06.103 --> 00:05:07.810
I'm the chef owner of Here's Looking at You,

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.040
and we're gonna try chicken liver mousse

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.450
and stracciatella cheese today.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.000
- What kind of establishment is Here's Looking at You?

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.060
- Well, we're like a progressive

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.430
kinda Southern California restaurant.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.810
The repertoire is French at heart,

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.900
but executed with all types of different cultures.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.970
- When did toast become, like a meal?

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.130
- Good bread is the vessel for everything

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.250
that you're trying to make. And it wasn't

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:27.110
until I met Andy from Bub and Grandma's,

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.020
our signature dishes involve Bub's bread.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.830
It's such a quintessential part of the restaurant.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.220
His bread has a perfect balance of texture and flavor.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.900
The aeration of it, and the fact he cooks

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.610
with passion that implements on the food.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.030
- [Andy] Part of our thing is creating

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.700
a happy place to work that doesn't

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.940
push people away from their families and friends,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.170
and it translates into the products that come out the door.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.700
We use higher percentage whole grain

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.160
than most other bakers in town.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.110
I feel like there's more flavor in the whole grains

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.110
and it helps to move people more towards real bread

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.210
as oppose to chemically engineered bread.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:55.630
It's a three day process, basically.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.960
We mix weighed out bins, they start fermenting.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.130
Throughout the three hour bulk fermentation,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.490
we do folds on the dough to build strength

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.530
These keep building a scaffold inside that

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.230
holds in all those CO2 bubbles,

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:07.880
so that it doesn't collapse in the oven.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.120
Shaping it into a bowl,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.870
goes in the baskets, goes in the walk-in,

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.870
come in the next morning, straight out of the baskets

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.550
they flip onto the loader, and then they bake.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.190
Max is gonna score these because when we do our final shape,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.440
the exterior of the loaves are made strong enough

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.900
to hold the thing up. So now we want to

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.100
actually release that tension so that they can rise

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.050
to their fullest potential in the oven.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.350
Caring is number one, like I'm just a really anxious person

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.120
and if things aren't perfect, they need to be.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.140
Which these guys do, it's personal, like

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.020
especially when you get up early in the morning like this

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.730
and sacrifice part of your normal life

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.533
to come work here, you have to care.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.010
- This bread is epic. You know, we hit it up

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.160
with a lot of olive oil to help get a really good char on it

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.290
and just slap it on the grill,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.410
We have a wood burning grill, burns at like, 700 degrees.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:46.243
A lot of our clients are like

00:06:46.243 --> 00:06:47.680
is it supposed to be burnt like this?

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:48.556
and it's like yeah.

00:06:48.556 --> 00:06:49.675
(laughs)

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.210
A lot of people don't realize that burning stuff

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.410
like that and not even smoking is a technique of cooking.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:54.530
The chicken liver mousse we use

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.190
is really high quality Jidori chicken livers.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.650
I actually take my maple syrup and smoke it

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.200
and then we add that to the liver.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.090
We serve it with a side of Bub's bread,

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.870
and then pickled pupiras

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.770
which are like Spanish basque peppers.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.430
- [Steven] Alright, we got this beautiful rosé,

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:07.280
- [Andrew] We do.

00:07:07.443 --> 00:07:08.280
- [Steven] Which I know you love the color of.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.113
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:07:09.234 --> 00:07:10.840
(glasses clink)

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.600
So this is our first build your own toast segment.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.160
You get to control your flavors a little bit more.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.600
Do you want to try the bread on its own?

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.650
- I actually really do.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.860
Cause' he was really talking up Andy's bread.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.124
Cheers, Toast.

00:07:22.124 --> 00:07:24.207
(laughs)

00:07:24.687 --> 00:07:26.520
- That was so awkward.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:31.293
(mellow music)

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:32.893
Oh yeah.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.333
Already.

00:07:36.333 --> 00:07:38.800
- Look at the aeration on that bread inside, it's just like

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.830
- Okay, you're just saying aeration

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:40.985
cause' you heard the chef say aeration.

00:07:40.985 --> 00:07:41.818
- Look.

00:07:41.818 --> 00:07:43.210
- Look at the texture, yeah, look at the bread.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:44.423
Alright, here it comes.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.423
You wanna get that nice and shmeared on.

00:07:49.649 --> 00:07:52.100
(bread crackles)

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:52.933
- Whoa.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.093
- Yeah.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.280
- I used to think that trains were the perfect vehicle.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.973
No, bread is the perfect vehicle of flavor.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.893
It almost tastes aged.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:02.833
- Yeah.

00:08:02.833 --> 00:08:03.666
(clicks tongue)

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:04.681
- Yeah, let me try that.

00:08:04.681 --> 00:08:05.929
(clicks tongue)

00:08:05.929 --> 00:08:06.970
- [Jonathan] The strat cheese is a cheese

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:08.436
that we get from Gioia, it's a local producer here.

00:08:08.436 --> 00:08:11.380
We make it as kind of like a Japanese pesto

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.470
so we use Japanese parsley, Shingiku,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:16.110
which is a chrysanthemum leaf, corilla, some cilantro,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.180
and then yuzu kosho, and mix that all together

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.260
like a pretty smooth pesto, put that on top of the cheese,

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:21.450
add toasted pumpkin seeds on top,

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:22.660
and then serve it with Bub's bread.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:26.253
^- [Steve] Round two, baby, we got a Chardonnay, also bubbly.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.280
- Do you know any foreign language toasts?

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.070
- Yeah, in like, Korean we go jjan.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:31.320
In Chinese, it's "gānbēi."

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:32.153
- Gānbēi.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.040
In Ukrainian it's "nazdorovje."

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.809
- Ooh. I like that. How do you say that?

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:38.094
Naz-ee-uh-ro-ga?

00:08:38.094 --> 00:08:39.530
- Naz-dah-rov-ee-uh

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:40.663
- Naz-duh-rov-ah

00:08:40.663 --> 00:08:41.820
- Naz-dah-rov-ee-uh

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.190
- You know what? Let's eat some toast.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:47.013
- So now we've upgraded to a deeper vessel.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.460
Hell yeah, we got soft cheese.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.593
That is the realm I like to live in.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.363
- [Steve] God, see, that's what you want in cheese.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.350
A little creamy, a little messy

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.030
- A little stringy, but it's a cool cheese.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.430
Don't think this is hot and melty.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.003
- Uh-uh, toast.

00:09:07.474 --> 00:09:10.224
(bread crackles)

00:09:11.777 --> 00:09:12.943
That's the one.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:14.203
- Yeah.

00:09:14.203 --> 00:09:15.550
- I'm gonna cry, it's so good.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.033
- This tastes like, both the milk fresh from the cow,

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:21.543
and the grass that the cow was standing on.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.450
In a very delicious way, not a gross way.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:24.283
- Mm-hm.

00:09:24.283 --> 00:09:25.780
- I think the amount the amount of time it takes to chew

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:28.010
a piece of bread really says something about how good it is.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.130
